    <briefdescription>
<para>C++20 concept satisfied when <computeroutput>T</computeroutput> is a range that the formatter writes element by element. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para><parameterlist kind="templateparam"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>T</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The type to be checked.</para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
The <computeroutput>formattable_range</computeroutput> concept is satisfied when <computeroutput>T</computeroutput> can be iterated by a range-based <computeroutput>for</computeroutput> statement, with <computeroutput>begin()</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>end()</computeroutput> members (<computeroutput>container_like</computeroutput>) or functions (<computeroutput>free_range</computeroutput>), and:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>is not an array, since the arrays are compared by address, and are written as addresses, as the pointers they decay to;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>is not a string (see <computeroutput>has_npos</computeroutput>), which is written as text;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>does not have elements of its own type, like <computeroutput>std::filesystem::path</computeroutput>, whose elements are paths, so that formatting it would recurse without end;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>has elements that satisfy <computeroutput>extension::printable</computeroutput>, so that a range of elements that cannot be reported is rejected where it is compared, not where its elements are formatted.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>The conditions are tested in this order, so the elements of a recursive range are not tested. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
